/-!
# The disjoint union of a standard covering

A standard covering `{Uᵢ → T}` of an affine scheme can be replaced by the single morphism
`∐ Uᵢ → T` it induces. The resulting one-member family generates the same covering sieve, so it
is a covering of `T` for each of the five standard topologies; it is moreover a covering family
in the fpqc sense, and in every topology other than Zariski it is a covering family for that
topology itself. Since the component inclusions refine the single-morphism family, the induced
map on Čech complexes of an abelian sheaf is an isomorphism.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.IsStandardCovering.sieve_sigmaDesc_mem_topology` is the uniform form of
  Stacks 03FI part (1): the sieve generated by `∐ Uᵢ → T` covers `T` in every one of the five
  topologies.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.IsStandardCovering.singleton_sigmaDesc_mem_fpqcPrecoverage` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.IsStandardCovering.singleton_sigmaDesc_mem_precoverage` are the
  family-level forms of part (1), for the fpqc topology and for the topology at hand.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.IsStandardCovering.sieve_sigmaDesc_mem_smallEtaleTopology` is part (1) on
  the small étale site of a scheme.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.isIso_cechComplexMap_coproductRefinement_over` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.isIso_cechComplexMap_coproductRefinement_smallEtale` are part (3), on the
  big site of a scheme and on its small étale site.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.IsStandardCovering.isAffine_sigmaObj` is Stacks 03OZ part (1): the
  disjoint union is an affine scheme; `AlgebraicGeometry.IsStandardCovering.isAffine_left_sigmaObj`
  is the same for the disjoint union taken in the big site over a base.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.zariskiTopology_le_grothendieckTopology` says the big Zariski
  topology is coarser than each of the five, which turns the comparison above into Stacks 03OZ
  part (3) for an abelian sheaf on the big `τ`-site:
  `AlgebraicGeometry.IsStandardCovering.isIso_cechComplexMap_coproductRefinement_bigSite`.

## Implementation notes

Part (1) holds for every one of the five topologies at the level of covering *sieves*, because
the sieve generated by `∐ Uᵢ → T` contains each `Uᵢ → T`, and `{Uᵢ → T}` is a covering family.
As a covering *family* it holds for every topology other than Zariski, where it fails: a finite
disjoint union of opens of `T` is not an open immersion into `T`, although the morphism from it
is flat, locally of finite presentation, syntomic, smooth or étale as soon as every member is.
Part (3) is unaffected by this, since the Čech complex of the one-member family only requires
the coproduct and its iterated fibre products to exist.

Finite disjoint unions exist in the small étale site of a scheme, which is what makes the Čech
comparison available there; the comparison is proved separately on the big site, since a
covering condition on an abstract category does not by itself make fibre products distribute
over coproducts. The scheme, index and coefficient universes are independent, and coefficients
are embedded by the fully faithful `AddCommGrpCat.uliftFunctor` into a universe admitting the
products the Čech complex requires.

Stacks 03OZ is the same statement in the étale-cohomology chapter, where it is phrased for an
abelian sheaf on one of the five big sites over a base or on the small étale site of a scheme,
and where affineness of the disjoint union is listed as a separate conclusion. Its part (2) is
Stacks 03FI part (1) in the two forms proved here, and its part (3) on the big `τ`-site is the
comparison above instantiated at the `τ`-topology, which is admissible because every open
immersion has the property of morphisms defining each of the five topologies.

